{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 2,
  "is_atomic": false,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this analysis — are we maximizing the total value of exports (i.e., the value of goods Carelland sells to the world market)?",
    "Is the value measured at the world-market prices you listed (steel 500, engines 1500, electronics 300, plastic 1200 Klunz per unit)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for the objective (maximizing export value) and separately confirms the specific pricing basis for that value. These are distinct pieces of information that could be answered independently."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "How exactly is domestic GDP defined in this model — is it the total value of all four goods (steel, engines, electronics, plastic) produced domestically, valued at the world-market prices you listed, minus the value of imported goods used in production?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for the specific definition of domestic GDP, including its components and valuation method."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 2,
  "is_atomic": false,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the four listed world-market prices (steel 500, engines 1500, electronics 300, plastic 1200 Klunz) the prices at which Carelland sells its exports?",
    "Are these the same prices used to value the imported goods that appear in the production input requirements?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks two distinct questions: one about the export selling prices and another about whether those same prices apply to imported inputs. These can be answered independently."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the \"Klunz imported goods\" in the production input requirements (e.g., 250 Klunz for steel, 300 for engines) a quantity of imported goods measured in Klunz (the currency), meaning each unit of output requires that many Klunz worth of imports, or are they a separate physical unit of imported goods that has its own price?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for the definition of the unit 'Klunz' in the context of production inputs, presenting two mutually exclusive interpretations."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Do the production input requirements (e.g., engines require 0.8 steel, 0.15 electronics, 0.11 plastic) refer to goods that must be produced domestically by Carelland, or can those inputs be satisfied by imports as well?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether production inputs must be domestic or can be imported."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there a limit on the total amount of imports Carelland can purchase (in Klunz), or are imports unlimited as long as the production and labor constraints are satisfied?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the existence of a limit on imports, presenting a binary choice (limited vs. unlimited) which constitutes one independent query."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the production input requirements (e.g., engines require 0.8 steel, 0.15 electronics, 0.11 plastic) satisfied by goods produced domestically by Carelland, meaning the model must ensure enough domestic production of each input to support the output levels?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether domestic production must satisfy input requirements, which is one semantically independent request."
}

